The Bible contains 12 specific individual case studies of exorcism (alongside several general summaries of mass deliverances), performed by Jesus, his disciples, and the Apostle Paul.
(Note: While David playing the harp in 1 Samuel 16:14–23 brought temporary relief to King Saul from a distressing spirit, it is generally categorized as spiritual soothing rather than a formal exorcism.)
Performed by Jesus
The Man in the Capernaum Synagogue (Mark 1:21–28; Luke 4:31–37): Jesus silences and casts out an unclean spirit during a Sabbath teaching.
The Gerasene (Gadarene) Demoniac (Matthew 8:28–34; Mark 5:1–20; Luke 8:26–39): Jesus casts a "Legion" of demons out of a man living among tombs and sends them into a herd of pigs. (Matthew mentions two men).
The Mute Demon-Possessed Man (Matthew 9:32–34): Jesus casts out a demon causing muteness, allowing the man to speak immediately.
The Blind and Mute Demon-Possessed Man (Matthew 12:22–23; Luke 11:14): Jesus delivers a man who was both blind and unable to speak, sparking the "Beelzebul controversy" with the Pharisees.
The Syrophoenician (Canaanite) Woman's Daughter (Matthew 15:21–28; Mark 7:24–30): Jesus casts a demon out of a Gentile girl from a distance in response to her mother's persistent faith.
The Boy with an Unclean Spirit (Matthew 17:14–21; Mark 9:14–29; Luke 9:37–43): After the disciples fail to cast out a spirit causing severe seizures, Jesus rebukes the demon and heals the boy.
The Woman with a Disabling Spirit (Luke 13:10–17): Jesus frees a woman who had been bent over for 18 years by a spirit of infirmity.
Mary Magdalene (Mark 16:9; Luke 8:2): Though the exact moment isn't narrated, Scripture notes that Jesus had cast seven demons out of her.
Performed by Others
An Unnamed Exorcist (Mark 9:38–41; Luke 9:49–50): John reports seeing someone outside their group successfully casting out demons in Jesus's name; Jesus tells them not to stop him.
The Twelve Disciples & The Seventy-Two (Mark 6:7–13; Luke 10:17): Sent out by Jesus with authority, the disciples successfully cast out many demons across surrounding towns.
Philip the Evangelist (Acts 8:6–7): While preaching in Samaria, unclean spirits come out of many people with loud shrieks through Philip's ministry.
The Apostle Paul (Acts 16:16–18): In Philippi, Paul commands a spirit of divination to leave an enslaved fortune-telling girl in the name of Jesus Christ.
Failed Attempt
The Seven Sons of Sceva (Acts 19:13–16): Jewish traveling exorcists attempt to use Jesus's name without personal faith and are violently overpowered by the possessed man.
